# Line-ending policy.
#
# GitHub Desktop on Windows normalises to LF when it commits, while the working
# files here are CRLF. Without this file git reports VERSION and the two vendored
# libraries as modified forever, so every commit carries spurious changes and real
# ones are harder to see.

# Normalise text in the repository, check out with native endings.
* text=auto

# Windows scripts need CRLF to run reliably.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Vendored third-party libraries: never normalise. They are not edited here, and
# rewriting their line endings only creates noise in diffs.
pdfmake.min.js -text
vfs_fonts.js   -text

# The two generated specifications name the SHA-256 hash of their own <style> block in
# their own CSP, because they are single files with no stylesheet to point at. A hash
# covers bytes, so translating their line endings on checkout changes the hash the
# browser computes and the document renders unstyled - and the release gate fails on a
# clean clone with nothing wrong in the source. Same rule as the libraries above: where
# the bytes are the contract, git must not rewrite them.
USER-SPECIFICATION.html      -text
DEVELOPER-SPECIFICATION.html -text

# A single-line stamp rewritten by bump-version.ps1 on Windows.
VERSION text eol=crlf
